banner1 for OS-9/68000======================ORIG holds banner-01 from the `banners' collection, comp.sources.unixvolume 26, issue 141 (V1.1, 1993) -- three files, verified against theposting byte for byte.NOTHING WAS CHANGED.  banner.c and font.c build exactly as posted, andthe files beside ORIG are copies of them.The reason is that this program came from here.  Its header says"Written in 1987 by Wolfgang Ocker, reccoware systems, Puchheim" and"Tested on OS-9/68000, SunOS", and the collection's own description ofit reads: "This was the very first banner on OS-9/68000 and it alsocompiled fine on Unix, so it was one of the few programs thattransitioned with me from OS-9/68000 to Unix."  So the OS-9 arms werealready in the source, and everything they call is here:  _errmsg()    reports an error the way OS-9 utilities do, with the               error number.  It is in clib.l, and five programs               already on this disk call it (indent, qt, xc among               them).  Under #ifndef OSK the same places use               fputs/fprintf to stderr instead.  intercept()  catches a signal; clib.l has it.  It is reached only by               the -l option, which exists under #ifdef OSK alone.  <modes.h>    included under OSK for S_IREAD, which the other arm               defines as 0.banner-12 in the same collection is this program's own descendant --1.00 Ocker 1987, then Frank Kaefer, then Paul E. Black -- and is underthe GPL v2.  It is not carried: the period original is what thiscollection prefers, and the two would be the same banner twice.The disk's `banner' is a different program: banner-04 of the samecollection, Brian Wallis's sysvbanner.  `cursive' is banner-05's, JanWolter's.  Neither is disturbed.Build: cc banner.c font.c -DOSK